# Heat Transfer Simulation (Python)

This project models **transient heat conduction in a cylindrical resistor** using two numerical methods:

- Explicit Finite Difference Method (FDM)
- Implicit Crank–Nicolson Method

It includes internal heat generation, convective boundary conditions, and visualisation of the temperature distribution over time.

---

## Overview

The simulation solves the **radial heat equation in cylindrical coordinates**, accounting for:

- Internal heat generation within the material  
- Symmetry at the centreline (Neumann boundary condition)  
- Convective heat loss at the outer surface (Robin boundary condition)  

The project demonstrates how different numerical schemes affect **stability, accuracy, and computational behaviour**.

---

## Methods Implemented

### Explicit Finite Difference Method
- Time-marching scheme  
- Simple and intuitive implementation  
- Stability governed by the **Courant condition**

### Crank–Nicolson Method (Implicit)
- Semi-implicit scheme (unconditionally stable)  
- Solves a system of linear equations at each timestep  
- More computationally intensive but significantly more stable  

---

## Features

- Cylindrical coordinate heat conduction model  
- Internal heat generation term  
- Convective boundary condition  
- Comparison of explicit vs implicit schemes  
- Visual outputs:
  - Radial temperature distribution  
  - 2D heatmaps  
  - Time evolution animation (MP4)

---

